 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 20,
   "y": 12,
   "text": "THE VILLAGE GREEN. A square of grass at the heart of Bushmills, kept in trim by a few hundred feet and the odd sheep who 'got in'. It has hosted every fair, concert and short argument for over two hundred years."
  },
  {
   "x": 28,
   "y": 28,
   "text": "THE OLD MILL. Bushmills is named for the mill on the river Bush: Muileann na Buaise, in Irish. A water mill has stood here for centuries, turning the river's weight into something useful and the walker's mood into 'oh, how quaint'."
  },
  {
   "x": 29,
   "y": 12,
   "text": "THE RIVER BUSH. A short, quick river full of salmon and trout, running down to the Causeway Coast. Anglers have cast here for centuries. The river has politely ignored them for just as long."
  },
  {
   "x": 3,
   "y": 15,
   "text": "BUSHMILLS. A small town with a river, a green, a steam train and a view. In 1883 the world's first hydro-electric tramway ran through here. The tram has gone. The feeling of being ahead of the times has not."
  }
 ],
